-- === Contingencies ===

-- Q192 [score:6] short-stem(10w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Manufacturing is named as a defendant in a product liability lawsuit filed in October 2025. The company''s legal counsel advises that it is probable Ridgemont will lose the case, and estimates the liability at $1.5 million. The controller is preparing the December 31, 2025 financial statements and asks whether the loss should be accrued or only disclosed. Under ASC 450, a loss contingency should be accrued when:',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 450-20-25, accrual of a loss contingency requires two conditions: (1) information available before the financial statements are issued indicates it is probable that an asset has been impaired or a liability has been incurred at the balance sheet date, and (2) the amount of the loss can be reasonably estimated. In Ridgemont''s case, both conditions are met — the loss is probable and estimated at $1.5 million — so a $1.5 million accrual is required. Choice A is incorrect because a "possible" loss does not meet the probability threshold — ASC 450 requires disclosure only when a loss is reasonably possible but not probable. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 450 uses the standard of "probable," not "certain" — waiting for certainty would understate liabilities and violate the conservatism principle.'
WHERE id = 192;

-- === Equity ===

-- Q441 [score:6] short-stem(10w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASC 718
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Corp. is designing its executive compensation plan for 2026 and is evaluating two equity-based instruments: restricted stock awards and restricted stock units (RSUs). The CFO asks the compensation committee to explain the key difference between these instruments for financial reporting purposes. Under ASC 718, restricted stock differs from restricted stock units in that:',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 718-10-25, restricted stock involves the actual issuance of shares to the employee at the grant date — the employee holds shares subject to forfeiture restrictions and typically receives dividends and voting rights during the vesting period. RSUs, by contrast, are contractual promises to deliver shares (or cash equivalent) upon vesting — the employee does not receive actual shares until the vesting conditions are satisfied. Both instruments are typically equity-classified and measured at grant-date fair value. Choice A is incorrect because restricted stock is generally equity-classified, not liability-classified — liability classification would apply only if the entity is obligated to settle in cash. Choice C is incorrect because it reverses the relationship: restricted stock holders typically have voting rights, while RSU holders do not receive shares (or voting rights) until vesting occurs.'
WHERE id = 441;

