-- === Governmental Accounting ===

-- Q177 [score:5] short-stem(8w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A newly hired staff accountant at the City of Ridgemont is preparing the general fund financial statements for the fiscal year ended June 30, 2026. She asks her supervisor whether the general fund should report long-term capital assets and long-term debt, as she is accustomed to full accrual accounting from her private-sector experience. The supervisor explains that governmental funds use a different measurement focus. Under GASB standards, governmental funds use the:',
  explanation = 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, governmental funds (general, special revenue, capital projects, debt service, and permanent funds) use the current financial resources measurement focus and modified accrual basis of accounting. This means they report on the sources, uses, and balances of current financial resources — essentially cash and items expected to be converted to cash soon. Long-term assets and long-term liabilities are excluded from governmental fund statements and reported only at the government-wide level. Choice B is incorrect because the economic resources measurement focus is used by proprietary funds, fiduciary funds, and the government-wide financial statements — not by governmental funds. Choice C is incorrect because governmental funds use modified accrual, not the cash basis — revenues are recognized when measurable and available, not solely when cash is received.'
WHERE id = 177;

-- Q178 [score:5] short-stem(11w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Thornfield levied $12 million in property taxes on January 1, 2026, for the fiscal year ending December 31, 2026. By year-end, $11.2 million has been collected, with an additional $500,000 expected to be collected in January 2026 and $300,000 in March 2026. The city''s availability period is 60 days. The finance director must determine how much property tax revenue to recognize in the general fund for fiscal year 2026. Under GASB standards, property tax revenue should be recognized:',
  explanation = 'Under modified accrual accounting as prescribed by GASB Statement No. 34, revenue is recognized when it is both measurable and available. For property taxes, revenue is recognized in the period for which the taxes are levied, provided the taxes are collected within the availability period (typically 60 days after year-end). In Thornfield''s case, the $11.2 million collected during the year plus the $500,000 collected in January (within 60 days) would be recognized as revenue, while the $300,000 collected in March (beyond 60 days) would be reported as deferred inflows of resources. Choice A is incorrect because revenue recognition is not triggered by mailing assessment notices — the levy date and collection timing determine recognition. Choice C is incorrect because modified accrual is not pure cash basis — taxes are recognized as revenue when available, even if not yet collected.'
WHERE id = 178;

-- Q179 [score:5] short-stem(11w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A GASB standards instructor is explaining to new government accounting staff at the City of Clearwater that the city must prepare two sets of financial statements — fund-level statements and government-wide statements — and that these statements use different bases of accounting. When preparing the government-wide statement of net position and statement of activities, the city must use the:',
  explanation = 'Under GASB Statement No. 34, the government-wide financial statements (statement of net position and statement of activities) use the economic resources measurement focus and the full accrual basis of accounting, similar to private-sector entities. This means revenues are recognized when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of cash timing. Capital assets are reported and depreciated, and long-term liabilities are recognized. Choice A is incorrect because the modified accrual basis is used in governmental fund financial statements, not in the government-wide statements — using modified accrual at the government-wide level would exclude long-term assets and liabilities. Choice B is incorrect because the cash basis only recognizes transactions when cash changes hands, which would fail to report significant receivables, payables, and accruals required for a complete financial picture.'
WHERE id = 179;

-- Q459 [score:6] short-stem(9w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite GASB 75
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The City of Barrington provides post-retirement health insurance benefits to eligible retired employees. The actuary engaged by the city is preparing the total OPEB liability calculation for the year ended June 30, 2026, and asks the finance director which actuarial cost method GASB requires. Under GASB Statement No. 75, the total OPEB liability must be measured using:',
  explanation = 'GASB Statement No. 75 (paragraph 22) requires the entry age normal cost method as the sole permissible actuarial cost method for measuring the total OPEB liability. This method spreads the actuarial present value of projected benefits as a level percentage of pay over each employee''s career from entry age to assumed exit age. Choice B is incorrect because GASB 75 does not permit a choice among actuarial methods — unlike older GASB standards, it prescribes a single method to ensure comparability across government employers. Choice C is incorrect because the projected unit credit method is required under FASB ASC 715 for private-sector employers, not under GASB standards — the two frameworks deliberately use different actuarial approaches.'
WHERE id = 459;

-- Q462 [score:5] short-stem(9w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The public works department of the City of Thornfield issues a $45,000 purchase order on September 15, 2026, for road maintenance equipment to be delivered in November. The city uses the encumbrance system for budgetary control in its general fund. The finance director records an entry at the time the purchase order is issued. Under governmental fund accounting, an encumbrance is recorded when:',
  explanation = 'Under governmental fund budgetary accounting, an encumbrance is recorded when a purchase order or contract is issued, representing a commitment of appropriated budgetary resources for a future expenditure. The entry debits Encumbrances and credits Budgetary Fund Balance — Reserved for Encumbrances. When the equipment is received in November, the encumbrance is reversed and an expenditure is recorded at the actual invoice amount. Encumbrances provide budgetary control by reserving funds and preventing overspending of appropriations. Choice A is incorrect because receipt of goods triggers reversal of the encumbrance and recognition of an expenditure, not the initial encumbrance entry. Choice D is incorrect because budget adoption creates the budgetary entry (debit Estimated Revenues, credit Appropriations), not an encumbrance — encumbrances arise from specific purchasing commitments during the year.'
