-- === Intangible Assets ===

-- Q161 [score:6] short-stem(9w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASC 730
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Pharmaceuticals spent $2.3 million during 2025 on laboratory research to identify compounds for a new cardiovascular drug. The project is in its early stages, and no compounds have advanced to clinical trials. The CFO is determining how to account for these costs in the year-end financial statements. Under ASC 730, research and development costs are generally:',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 730-10-25, research and development costs are expensed as incurred because the future economic benefits are uncertain at the time the costs are incurred. Thornfield must expense the entire $2.3 million in 2025. Limited exceptions exist: R&D assets acquired in a business combination are capitalized at fair value under ASC 805, and software development costs are capitalized after technological feasibility is established under ASC 985-20. Choice A is incorrect because internally generated R&D costs cannot be capitalized as intangible assets — the uncertainty of future benefits fails to meet the asset recognition criteria. Choice B is incorrect because deferral and amortization would require reliable measurement of the benefit period, which is not possible during the research phase when the outcome remains uncertain.'
WHERE id = 161;

-- Q416 [score:5] short-stem(5w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite IAS 38
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont BioTech, a company reporting under IFRS, spent EUR 1.8 million during 2025 on basic research to explore potential applications of a new polymer compound. The project has not yet reached the development phase. The controller must determine the appropriate accounting treatment for these expenditures. Under IAS 38, research phase costs are:',
  explanation = 'Under IAS 38.54, research costs must always be expensed as incurred because during the research phase the entity cannot demonstrate that an intangible asset exists that will generate probable future economic benefits. Only development costs may be capitalized, and only when all six criteria in IAS 38.57 are met: technical feasibility, intention to complete, ability to use or sell, probable future economic benefits, availability of resources, and reliable measurement of costs. Choice C is incorrect because the six development criteria apply exclusively to the development phase — costs incurred during the research phase cannot be capitalized regardless of whether any criteria are met. Choice D is incorrect because IAS 38 does not permit arbitrary deferral and amortization of research costs over a fixed period — the standard explicitly requires immediate expensing.'
