-- === Inventory ===

-- Q394 [score:5] short-stem(8w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Retailers experienced a warehouse fire that destroyed its inventory records. The controller decides to estimate ending inventory using the gross profit method, applying the company''s historical gross profit margin of 40% to net sales of $800,000. An accounting intern asks whether net markdowns should be factored into the estimate. Under the gross profit method, net markdowns are:',
  explanation = 'The gross profit method estimates ending inventory using only cost data — it does not use retail amounts at any point in the calculation. The method works by subtracting estimated cost of goods sold (net sales multiplied by the cost ratio, derived from the historical gross profit percentage) from cost of goods available for sale. Net markdowns are a concept relevant only to the retail inventory method, where goods are tracked at both cost and retail amounts. In Clearwater''s case, estimated COGS is $800,000 x 60% = $480,000. Choice A is incorrect because the gross profit method has no retail column — subtracting markdowns from retail is a step in the retail inventory method, not the gross profit method. Choice D is incorrect because the gross profit percentage is based on historical cost and sales data, not adjusted by current-period retail markdowns.'
WHERE id = 394;

-- === Leases ===

-- Q164 [score:6] short-stem(10w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASC 842
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont LLC enters into a lease for office equipment on January 1, 2026, with a lease term of 10 months and no purchase option. The controller asks whether the company can avoid recognizing a right-of-use asset and lease liability for this lease. Under ASC 842, the short-term lease exemption is available for leases with a term at or below:',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 842-20-25-2, a lessee may elect a practical expedient to not recognize right-of-use assets and lease liabilities for leases with a term of 12 months or less at commencement, provided the lease does not include a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise. Ridgemont''s 10-month lease qualifies for this exemption, and payments would be recognized as expense on a straight-line basis over the lease term. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 842 uses a 12-month threshold, not 6 months — a 6-month cutoff would be overly restrictive and is not part of the standard. Choice B is incorrect because 36 months significantly exceeds the short-term lease exemption threshold and would require full recognition of a right-of-use asset and lease liability.'
WHERE id = 164;

-- Q166 [score:5] absolute-assurance-trap -> replace trap distractors with realistic misconceptions
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = '["Rental income recognized on a straight-line basis and the underlying asset retained on the balance sheet","A net investment in the lease and a lease receivable, with no derecognition of the underlying asset","A right-of-use asset and a corresponding lease liability measured at present value of payments","A net investment in the lease, any selling profit or loss, and derecognition of the underlying asset"]',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 842-30-25-1, in a sales-type lease the lessor derecognizes the underlying asset from its balance sheet, recognizes a net investment in the lease (comprising the lease receivable and any unguaranteed residual asset), and recognizes selling profit or loss at commencement — measured as the difference between the fair value of the underlying asset and its carrying amount. This treatment reflects that the lessor has effectively transferred control of the asset to the lessee. Choice A is incorrect because straight-line rental income and retaining the asset describe operating lease treatment under ASC 842-30-25-11, not sales-type lease treatment. Choice B is incorrect because a defining feature of a sales-type lease is derecognition of the underlying asset — retaining it on the balance sheet contradicts the economics of the transaction.'
WHERE id = 166;

-- Q418 [score:6] absolute-assurance-trap -> replace trap distractors with realistic misconceptions
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = '["The present value of lease payments equals or exceeds substantially all of the fair value of the asset","The lease term covers at least 50% of the asset''s total remaining economic life at commencement","The lessee has provided a residual value guarantee to the lessor covering any decline in asset value","The lessor has incurred significant initial direct costs in negotiating and arranging the lease"]',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 842-10-25-2, a lessor classifies a lease as sales-type if any of five criteria are met: (1) the lease transfers ownership, (2) there is a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise, (3) the lease term is for the major part of the remaining economic life, (4) the present value of lease payments and any residual value guarantee by the lessee equals or exceeds substantially all of the fair value, or (5) the asset is so specialized it has no alternative use to the lessor. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 842 uses "major part" of economic life (generally interpreted as 75%), not 50% — a 50% threshold would capture too many leases that do not represent effective transfers of control. Choice C is incorrect because a residual value guarantee alone does not trigger sales-type classification — it is only factored into the present value calculation under criterion (4).'
WHERE id = 418;

-- Q419 [score:5] absolute-assurance-trap -> replace trap distractors with realistic misconceptions
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = '["A sales-type lease because the lessee obtains substantially all economic benefits from the asset","A direct financing lease because the present value test is partially met at the 60% threshold","An operating lease because none of the five sales-type criteria or direct financing criteria are met","A finance lease under ASC 842 because the lease term exceeds one year at commencement"]',
  explanation = 'Since none of the five sales-type criteria are met — no ownership transfer, no purchase option, 3 out of 10 years is not the major part of economic life, 60% is not substantially all of fair value, and the asset is not specialized — the lease is not classified as sales-type. It also does not meet direct financing criteria (which require the present value test or economic life test to be met, plus no selling profit, and collectibility is probable). Therefore, the lessor classifies this as an operating lease under ASC 842-10-25-3, recognizing rental income on a straight-line basis over the lease term. Choice A is incorrect because none of the five sales-type classification criteria are satisfied — "obtaining economic benefits" is not itself a classification criterion. Choice B is incorrect because 60% of fair value does not meet the "substantially all" threshold required under criterion (4), and direct financing also requires the present value or economic life test to be met.'
