-- === Not-for-Profit Accounting ===

-- Q184 [score:5] short-stem(11w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASU 2016-14
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The board of directors of the Ridgemont Community Foundation, a not-for-profit organization, asks the finance director to present a more detailed breakdown of expenses in the annual financial statements. The finance director explains that current standards already require a specific format for expense reporting. Under ASU 2016-14, a not-for-profit organization must report expenses by:',
  explanation = 'Under ASU 2016-14 (ASC 958-720-45), not-for-profit organizations must report expenses by both their functional classification (program services, management and general, fundraising) and their natural classification (salaries, rent, depreciation, supplies). This dual presentation can appear on the face of the statement of activities, in a separate statement of functional expenses, or in the notes to the financial statements. The requirement ensures that financial statement users can evaluate both the purposes for which resources were spent and the types of costs incurred. Choice A is incorrect because reporting expenses by function only would omit the natural classification detail that helps users understand the types of costs incurred within each program. Choice B is incorrect because reporting by nature only would omit the functional classification needed to evaluate how much is spent on programs versus supporting services like administration and fundraising.'
WHERE id = 184;

-- Q467 [score:5] short-stem(11w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASU 2018-08
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Clearwater Arts Foundation receives a $500,000 pledge from a corporate donor. The pledge agreement states that funds will be released only after the Foundation secures matching donations of $500,000 from other sources, and if the match is not achieved within two years, the donor has no obligation to pay. The controller must determine whether this pledge represents a conditional or unconditional contribution. Under ASC 958, as clarified by ASU 2018-08, a contribution is considered conditional if it includes:',
  explanation = 'Under ASU 2018-08 (ASC 958-605-25-5A), a conditional contribution has two essential characteristics: (1) a barrier — a measurable performance-related or other condition that must be overcome (such as the matching requirement in Clearwater''s pledge agreement), and (2) a right of return of assets transferred or a release of the promisor from the obligation if the barrier is not met (the donor has no obligation if the match is not achieved). The Foundation would not recognize revenue until the $500,000 matching condition is satisfied. Choice B is incorrect because a restriction on how funds are used (e.g., "for scholarship purposes only") is a donor restriction under ASC 958-605, not a condition — restricted contributions are recognized as revenue when received. Choice D is incorrect because a reporting requirement is an administrative obligation, not a substantive barrier — the donor retains no right of return simply because a report is not filed.'
WHERE id = 467;

-- Q468 [score:6] length-cuing -> balance choice lengths
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = '["Are donated by a licensed professional such as an attorney, physician, or certified public accountant","Are donated during the entity''s fiscal year and exceed a de minimis fair value threshold","Exceed $5,000 in total fair value when aggregated across all volunteer hours during the period","Create or enhance a nonfinancial asset, or require specialized skills that would need to be purchased"]',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 958-605-25-16, contributed services are recognized as revenue only if they (1) create or enhance a nonfinancial asset (such as constructing a building), or (2) require specialized skills (e.g., accounting, legal, medical), are provided by individuals possessing those skills, and would typically need to be purchased if not donated. General volunteer services — such as ushering, serving food, or filing papers — do not meet these criteria and are not recognized as revenue. Choice A is incorrect because the standard focuses on whether services require specialized skills, not whether the donor holds a specific professional license — an unlicensed person providing skilled carpentry could qualify. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 958 establishes no dollar threshold for contributed services — recognition depends on the nature of the services, not their aggregate value.'
