-- === Revenue Recognition ===

-- Q150 [score:5] short-stem(11w) + length-cuing -> expand stem with scenario, balance choices
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Construction enters into a $6 million contract to build a custom warehouse for Barrington Logistics on Barrington''s property. Construction will span 18 months. As work progresses, Barrington controls the partially completed structure. The controller must determine whether revenue should be recognized over time or at a point in time. Under ASC 606, a performance obligation is satisfied over time when:',
  choices = '["The customer makes the final payment upon delivery and formal acceptance of the completed asset","The contract reaches its scheduled completion date and both parties sign a certificate of acceptance","The entity signs the contract and both parties agree to the transaction price and scope of work","The customer receives benefits as work is performed, or the asset has no alternative use with payment rights"]',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 606-10-25-27, a performance obligation is satisfied over time if any one of three criteria is met: (1) the customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits as the entity performs (e.g., routine cleaning services), (2) the entity''s performance creates or enhances a customer-controlled asset (as in Thornfield''s case — Barrington controls the warehouse during construction on its property), or (3) the entity''s performance does not create an asset with alternative use to the entity and the entity has an enforceable right to payment for performance completed to date. Choice A is incorrect because conditioning revenue on final payment describes point-in-time recognition, not over-time recognition — under over-time recognition, revenue is recognized as work progresses. Choice B is incorrect because reaching the completion date is a point-in-time event, and ASC 606 over-time criteria focus on the pattern of benefit transfer, not contract milestones.'
WHERE id = 150;

-- Q153 [score:6] short-stem(11w) -> expand stem with scenario, cite ASC 606
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Clearwater Software enters into a three-year license and maintenance contract with Ridgemont Financial for $900,000. Six months into the contract, Ridgemont requests additional customization features and an extended support term. Both parties agree to increase the contract price by $150,000. The controller must determine whether this change constitutes a contract modification. Under ASC 606, a contract modification is:',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 606-10-25-10, a contract modification is a change to the scope or price (or both) of a contract that is approved by the parties to the contract. It creates new or changes existing enforceable rights and obligations. Clearwater''s agreed-upon expansion of scope and $150,000 price increase is a contract modification that must be evaluated to determine whether it should be treated as a separate contract or as a modification of the existing contract. Choice A is incorrect because a change in the transaction price due to variable consideration (such as a performance bonus or penalty) is a remeasurement of the original contract terms, not a modification — no new approval is required. Choice B is incorrect because exercising an existing option was already contemplated in the original contract terms and does not create new enforceable rights — it activates pre-existing rights.'
WHERE id = 153;

-- === Statement of Cash Flows ===

-- Q190 [score:5] short-stem(10w) -> expand stem with scenario
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Industries reported several transactions during the year ended December 31, 2025: it purchased a patent for $400,000, collected a $200,000 note receivable from a customer, paid $150,000 in dividends to shareholders, and received $75,000 in interest on short-term investments. The controller is preparing the statement of cash flows and must classify each transaction. Which of the following transactions is classified as a financing activity?',
  explanation = 'Under ASC 230-10-45, financing activities involve transactions with owners and creditors of the entity that affect its capital structure, including issuing and repurchasing stock, borrowing and repaying debt, and paying dividends to shareholders. Thornfield''s $150,000 dividend payment is a financing activity because it represents a distribution of earnings to equity holders. Choice A is incorrect because purchasing a patent is an investing activity — it represents the acquisition of a long-lived asset that will generate future economic benefits. Choice B is incorrect because collecting a note receivable is an investing activity — it represents the recovery of principal on a lending arrangement, which involves deploying resources in assets outside the entity''s primary operations.'
WHERE id = 190;
